<!-- SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS -->
<div class="section" id="conclusions">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>

  <h3>Finding 1: Unprecedented Concentration</h3>
  <p>A single operator (SpaceX/Starlink) controls <strong>67.8%</strong> of all active satellites. This level of orbital dominance raises questions for spectrum regulators, debris-mitigation policymakers, and competition authorities. Any operational anomaly in the Starlink fleet — whether a firmware bug causing uncontrolled deorbits or a collision cascade — would affect two-thirds of all active spacecraft.</p>

  <h3>Finding 2: The Post-2020 Explosion</h3>
  <p><strong>90.5% of all active satellites were launched in the past six years.</strong> The fleet grew from ~1,400 objects at end of 2019 to nearly 15,000 by Q1 2026 — a &gt;10× increase in just over six years. 2025 was the single largest launch year on record (4,329 active satellites).</p>

  <h3>Finding 3: LEO Is the New Frontier</h3>
  <p>Low Earth Orbit now hosts <strong>94.6%</strong> of the active fleet, up from an estimated ~65% a decade ago. The 550-km shell alone (mean motion ~15.28 rev/day) contains thousands of Starlink satellites. This concentration creates congestion hotspots that demand active traffic management and conjunction assessment at unprecedented scale.</p>

  <h3>Finding 4: Inclination Clustering</h3>
  <p>The inclination distribution is heavily bimodal: a dominant peak at ~53° (Starlink primary shell) and a secondary peak at ~97° (sun-synchronous orbit). Between them, the 60°–80° range is relatively sparse, representing an opportunity for future constellations seeking to minimize conjunction risk.</p>

  <h3>Finding 5: Policy Implications</h3>
  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; color: #475569; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>Spectrum allocation:</strong> With 10,000+ Starlink satellites and 651 OneWeb satellites sharing LEO broadband spectrum, coordination and interference management are critical.</li>
    <li><strong>Debris mitigation:</strong> The sheer number of objects in the 500–600 km band requires robust deorbit compliance monitoring. Even a 1% failure-to-deorbit rate would leave ~100 uncontrolled objects.</li>
    <li><strong>Constellation licensing:</strong> Future mega-constellation applicants (Amazon Kuiper, Telesat Lightspeed, various Chinese proposals) will face an environment already shaped by a dominant incumbent.</li>
    <li><strong>Space situational awareness:</strong> Tracking 14,875 active objects — plus tens of thousands of debris fragments — requires continuous investment in ground-based and space-based surveillance.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

